Problem 4, sliding-window limiter. I'll keep a queue of the timestamps I've allowed, and since the input is sorted I can just drop from the front anything at or before t minus window. If the queue length is still under limit, allow it and push; otherwise reject. That's O(n) time and space bounded by limit, not by the number of requests.

```javascript
function allowed_requests(timestamps, limit, window) {
  const allowed = [];
  const result = [];
  let head = 0;

  for (const t of timestamps) {
    const cutoff = t - window;
    while (head < allowed.length && allowed[head] <= cutoff) {
      head++;
    }
    if (allowed.length - head < limit) {
      allowed.push(t);
      result.push(true);
    } else {
      result.push(false);
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

The `head` index avoids shifting the array, so each timestamp is pushed once and skipped once. Space is at most limit plus one, since we never hold more than limit allowed timestamps.

[[GIST]] queue of allowed times, O(n) time

A stack of expected closers. Push each opener, and on a closer check the top matches and pop, otherwise return False. At the end the stack must be empty.

```python
def is_balanced(s):
    pairs = {')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{'}
    stack = []
    for ch in s:
        if ch in '([{':
            stack.append(ch)
        elif ch in pairs:
            if not stack or stack.pop() != pairs[ch]:
                return False
    return not stack
```

One pass, O(n) time, O(n) space in the worst case.

